  "Number": 9,
  "Question": "Three students, with different names, line up single file. What is the probability that they are in alphabetical order from front-to-back?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{12}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{9}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{6}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Use the usual random-lineup interpretation, in which every order is equally likely. Label the students \\(X,Y,Z\\) in alphabetical order of their names. Their possible orders from front to back are\n\\[\nXYZ,\\ XZY,\\ YXZ,\\ YZX,\\ ZXY,\\ ZYX.\n\\]\nThis list is complete: each of the three students appears first in two listed orders, covering both ways to arrange the remaining students. No order appears twice.\n\nOnly \\(XYZ\\) is alphabetical. Thus the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{alphabetical orders}}{\\text{all orders}}=\\frac16.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\frac16\\), choice C.",
